<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/14th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Morgarten</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1315">1315</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Morgarten was fought on 15 November 1315 near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-ageri">Lake Ägeri</a>, where men of Schwyz and Uri ambushed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> army under Duke Leopold I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> on a narrow path between a steep slope and marshland. The heavily armed knights were routed. The victory secured the independence of the forest cantons, who renewed their alliance at Brunnen weeks later.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-morgarten.jpg" alt="Battle of Morgarten" width="320" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bendicht Tschachtlan. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bendicht_Tschachtlan,_Die_Schlacht_am_Morgarten_(c._1470).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Toward the end of the 13th century the House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> coveted the area around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotthard-pass">Gotthard Pass</a>, which offered the shortest passage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Uri, Schwyz and Unterwalden, which had formalised the Swiss Confederacy in 1291, held imperial freedom letters granting them local autonomy. In 1314 Louis IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and the Habsburg prince Frederick the Handsome each claimed the imperial crown, and the Confederates supported Louis. War broke out after men of Schwyz under Werner Stauffacher raided the Habsburg-protected Einsiedeln Abbey on the night of 6 January 1314, following a dispute over land that settlers had cleared. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Constance excommunicated Schwyz, and then Uri and Unterwalden.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Frederick's brother Leopold led a large army against the Confederates. The chronicler Johannes von <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winterthur">Winterthur</a> puts it at 20,000, probably an exaggeration, while Hans Delbrück estimated only 2,000 to 3,000 men, mainly well-trained and well-equipped knights. The size of the Confederate force is unknown, with estimates from 1,500 to 3,000 or 4,000. Leopold sent diversionary detachments and then chose to march to Sattel from the north. According to Konrad Justinger's chronicle of about 1430, the lords of Hünenberg warned Schwyz with arrows carrying a message, beware near Morgarten.</p><p>The men of Schwyz, supported by 600 men of Uri and Unterwalden, prepared an ambush between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-ageri">Lake Ägeri</a> and Sattel, where a narrow path ran between a steep slope and a swamp. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> column was stretched out for about 2 km and its vanguard met a roadblock near Schafstetten. The Confederates attacked, throwing rocks from above to frighten the horses and fighting the mounted knights with halberds. About 1,500 men were said to have been killed, not counting those drowned in the lake.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In December 1315 the Confederates renewed the oath of 1291, and in March 1316 Louis IV confirmed the rights of the Forest Cantons. A truce of ten months was agreed with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> in 1318. By 1323 the Forest Cantons had allied with Bern, and within 40 years Lucerne, Zug and Zürich had joined the Confederacy. The peace with the Habsburgs lasted until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sempach">Battle of Sempach</a> in 1386.</p><p>As the Confederacy's first military success, Morgarten became a staple of Swiss patriotism. A chapel at the site is recorded in 1501, and the present one dates to 1604. A monument was inaugurated in 1908 at Hauptsee, which was renamed Morgarten. A target shooting event, the Morgartenschiessen, has been held every year since 1912.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Morgarten” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the three communities defeated a Habsburg army under Duke Leopold I at the <b>Battle of Morgarten</b> in 1315, they renewed their alliance at Brunnen.</blockquote><a href="/empire/old-swiss-confederacy">Old Swiss Confederacy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1291 they joined Uri and Unterwalden in the Federal Charter, and in 1315 they defeated a Habsburg army at <b>Morgarten</b>, securing their independence and lending their name to the whole Swiss Confederacy.</blockquote><a href="/state/canton-of-schwyz">Canton of Schwyz</a></li><li><blockquote>After 1282 the family centred on Vienna, and the Swiss lands slipped away to the Swiss Confederacy, notably after defeats at <b>Morgarten</b> in 1315 and Sempach in 1386.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-habsburg">County of Habsburg</a></li></ul></section>
